    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d439">

                <pb facs="1208" n="1192" sameAs="frus:frus1951v01/pg1192"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">460.509/9–2751: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in France
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d439-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-09-27T20:00:00-04:00">September 27,
                            1951—8 p. m</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1849. Excon. During course brief discussions East-West trade at NATO Council mtg
                        Ottawa,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d439-f2"/> Secy made gen explanatory statement
                    of US policy reaffirming US belief that policy of selective embargo was the
                    correct one in present circumstances and that continued multilateral approach to
                    problem highly desirable. Secy also referred to current discussions in COCOM and
                    expressed hope that progress cld be made in bringing about greater parallelism
                    US and Internati List 1 and adequate control List 2 items.</p>
                <p>In more complete discussion East-West trade problem which Linder had with
                        Gaitskell,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d439-f3"/> Linder outlined present US legis
                    situation and expressed hope that Br wld see their way to agree to inclusion on
                    List 1 of those 1–A items in which their trade not significant in terms balance
                    of payments rather than adhering to a rigid interpretation of <pb facs="1209"
                        n="1193" xml:id="pg1193"/> strategic definition. Gaitskell appeared consider
                    this cld be done and promised look into matter on return London.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d439-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d439-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by Camp and cleared with Linder; repeated to London.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d439-f2" n="2" xml:id="d439-f2-content">
                <p>During the Seventh Session of the North Atlantic Council which met in Ottawa,
                    September 15–20, the subject of East-West trade was only briefly discussed as
                    noted in the source text and no resolutions concerning export controls were
                    considered. For documentation on the Seventh Session, including summaries of the
                    meetings, see <ref target="frus1951v03p1#pg_616">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, pp. 616</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d439-f3" n="3" xml:id="d439-f3-content">
                <p>The conversation under reference has not been further identified.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
